WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.933
A necessary assumption is something the argument needs in order for its reasoning to work.

2
00:00:06.233 --> 00:00:08.772
It need not supply a complete proof on its own.

3
00:00:09.072 --> 00:00:11.248
Only registered entrants may compete.

4
00:00:11.548 --> 00:00:15.260
The author concludes that Mara may compete because she has a pass.

5
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.717
For the conclusion to be true, Mara must be registered.

6
00:00:19.017 --> 00:00:23.327
If she were not registered, the conclusion would violate the stated requirement.

7
00:00:23.627 --> 00:00:33.483
But registration alone does not guarantee permission: the premise says only registered entrants may compete, not that every registered entrant may compete.

8
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:38.113
Mara’s registration is necessary without being sufficient on its own.

9
00:00:38.413 --> 00:00:44.664
Conditional arguments tempt us to choose a useful bridge even when the question asks what is required.

10
00:00:44.964 --> 00:00:49.615
Test the question’s demand instead of treating all assumption questions alike.

11
00:00:49.915 --> 00:00:53.797
Ask what must be accepted for this premise to serve as the author’s reason.

12
00:00:54.097 --> 00:00:57.575
Keep the candidate’s actual scope rather than strengthening it.

13
00:00:57.875 --> 00:01:00.755
Keep the candidate’s wording in view while negating it.

14
00:01:01.055 --> 00:01:10.207
If you used most, none or the reverse arrow where the actual denial requires only one counterexample, restart the test before judging the answer.
